<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    Doc 36 § F7 wave 5. The zone panel's derived-numbers block: what a resolution actually buys, and
    what the kernel's own rule refuses.

    ⚠ **A part rather than a panel, for the reason the panel ledger gives.** Both water panels build
    directly into a `DockPanel`, and `dock-panel.scrolls > * { flex-shrink: 0 }` reaches direct
    children only — a component wrapped round the whole panel inserts a box the rule cannot reach and
    a tall panel compresses instead of scrolling. A part whose host tag is an element the panel
    already creates changes nothing: `panel.Add<WaterZoneFacts>()` stands exactly where
    `panel.Add("water-zone-facts")` stood.

    ⚠ **`@inherits Vixen.Ui.UiElement` and not `Control`**, because a `Control` gives itself
    `variant-default` and `size-md` and the plain element this replaces has neither.

    ⚠ **The refusal is an interpolation and the facts are not, and the asymmetry is the tree's.**
    `<water-refusal>@Refusal</water-refusal>` appends a `text` child, which is exactly what
    `facts.Add("water-refusal").Add("text").Text = why` did — so here the markup spelling is the
    natural one. A `fact-name`'s own `Text` has no markup spelling at all, which is why the rows go
    through `FactRow`. Ledger shape 5, read in both directions.

    ⚠ **Two signals and no revision counter.** `WaterZoneSettings` is a plain settings object with no
    change notification, so the panel is told by `InspectorView.ValueChanged` and hands over the
    snapshot it computed. `Show` writes both at once, which is what keeps the numbers and the refusal
    from ever describing two different states.
-->
@component WaterZoneFacts
@namespace Vixen.Editor.Water
@inherits Vixen.Ui.UiElement
@tag water-zone-facts
@using System.Collections.Immutable
@using Vixen.Editor.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui
@using Vixen.Ui.Reactive

@for (var fact in Rows) {
    <FactRow key="@fact" Name="@fact.Label" Value="@fact.Value" />
}

@if (Refusal.Length > 0) {
    <water-refusal>@Refusal</water-refusal>
}

@code {
    /// <inheritdoc cref="Show" />
    readonly Signal<ImmutableArray<WaterFactLine>> rows = new([]);

    /// <inheritdoc cref="Show" />
    readonly Signal<string> refusal = new(string.Empty);

    /// <summary>What the block says, in order.</summary>
    public IReadOnlyList<WaterFactLine> Facts => rows.Value;

    /// <summary>And what it says the settings refuse, or empty.</summary>
    public string Refused => refusal.Value;

    /// <summary>Replaces the block.</summary>
    /// <param name="facts">The rows, in the order they should read.</param>
    /// <param name="why">What the settings refuse, or <see langword="null" /> for nothing.</param>
    /// <remarks>
    ///     ⚠ <b>Redrawn on every change rather than behind a button</b>, because the whole point of
    ///     the readout is that it is there <i>while</i> the numbers are being typed. A "recompute"
    ///     button would put the four-megabyte answer one press away from the person who was about to
    ///     press Create.
    /// </remarks>
    public void Show(IEnumerable<(string Label, string Value)> facts, string? why) {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(facts);

        rows.Value = [.. facts.Select((fact, slot) => new WaterFactLine(slot, fact.Label, fact.Value))];
        refusal.Value = why ?? string.Empty;
    }

    ImmutableArray<WaterFactLine> Rows => rows.Value;

    string Refusal => refusal.Value;
}
